// Gauntlet program 5 — formatted output — on the NATIVE Go target.
//
// The only gauntlet program with an effect, and the only one whose pass
// condition is not a number. Three things at once:
//
//   1. A host binding costs nothing. `fmt.Println` is one line in
//      targets/go/fmt.oro and no Go at all.
//   2. Order survives reduction. The three lines must print in the order
//      written, once each, however aggressively beta rewrites around them.
//   3. A pure computation feeds an effectful call, and neither moves the other:
//      `dot` must still fuse into one loop with no intermediate vector, while
//      sitting inside a `seq`.
//
// `seq` is sugar for a beta-redex with an unused binder and survives only
// because weakening is denied for an impure argument (effects.md §5). That rule
// is doing the work here: `fmt.Println` declares no `pure`, so its result — used
// zero times — cannot be dropped.
//
// The portable form was examples/report.oro, deleted with the portable layer
// (portable-2026-09-29; in git at 870de46). It took its array as a parameter,
// which `main` cannot do. Construction is why: a program could not build data at
// all until 2026-08-15, and here it is the language's `build` with a fill loop
// (construction.md; `go.make-float64` and `go.set-float64` until
// gotarget-2026-09-30).
use go
use go/fmt

fn fill(n) {
    var d := array { 0 : _ ∈ 0..n-1 }
    loop i = 0 { go.`>=`(i, n) -> d; else -> { d[i] := go.float64_of_int(i); again(go.`+`(i, 1)) } }
}

fn dot(a, b) {
    loop acc = 0.0, i = 0 {
        go.`>=`(i, len(a)) -> acc
        else -> again(go.`f+`(acc, go.`f*`(a[i], b[i])), go.`+`(i, 1))
    }
}

export fn main() {
    xs = fill(1000)
    fmt.Println("report")
    fmt.Println(len(xs))
    fmt.Println(dot(xs, xs))
}
